--- body ---
## Context

Author directive, 2026-10-27 (option C, no GitHub): merge first on cheap
checks, test main right after, Trust, Verify, Monitor. This replaces the
2026-10-26 design (a fast path for bookkeeping merges only, with a lag
counter, an age bound, coalescing and a verdict notes ref). Its PR 1676 was
parked for redesign after thirteen gaze rounds, most of them closing races in
that machinery.

Evidence, padme, 2026-10-27: a gate record runs the full suite on the merge
candidate in 88 to 95 s (READ). On a busy main the candidate moves during the
run: PR 1703's merge missed twice because other sessions merged during each
record. `make lint` runs in 6 to 15 s (READ).

## Design

- **Before the merge:** only the cheap checks `.merge-gate.json` declares for
  that moment (ticket validator, agnostic and pipefail guards, catalog drift,
  `make lint`), recorded on the merge candidate as today. Seconds, not
  minutes, so the candidate rarely moves under it.
- **After the merge: one runner, always on the newest tip.** The merging
  session starts the runner in the background unless one is already running
  (a lock file decides). The runner runs the full declared set (the test
  suite included) on the current main tip and records the result as a note
  on that tip, in the record format of today. When it ends, if main has
  moved, it runs once more on the newest tip, until main is quiet. A burst
  of N merges costs two runs, not N; a result stays valid for the tip it
  ran on, so a tip passed over is covered by the next green one.
- **Red after the merge:** a ticket is opened naming the tip, the failing
  checks, and the merges since the last green tip. Nothing reverts
  automatically.
- **No result after the merge** (the session died): the daily audit of main
  (`scripts/main_sweep.py`) reports tips that have no after-merge record and
  runs the set on the latest one.
- **Declared in one place.** `.merge-gate.json` says which checks run before
  and which after; it is read from main, as today.

Out: the bookkeeping fast path, max lag, age bound, coalescing worker,
verdict ref, flaky re-run and culprit search of the 2026-10-26 design. No
forge feature (merge queue, required checks) is used.

